    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty---School-of-Professional---Continuing-Studies_JR100016": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - School of Professional & Continuing Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-01-03\nPart time\n\nThis posting is set up to receive an application for adjunct faculty already identified by University of Richmond’s School of Professional & Continuing Studies. We are not currently recruiting for these positions, and you should   only apply if you have been specifically instructed to   by either University of Richmond’s Human Resource Department or the School of Professional & Continuing Studies. Adjunct faculty members in the School of Professional & Continuing Studies teach courses that draw upon their rich variety of academic disciplines and work experiences.  All part-time faculty must meet SACSCOC (Southern Association of Colleges and Schools – Commission on Colleges) requirements.  For the SPCS undergraduate programs, an individual must have earned either a master’s degree in their respective teaching field or a master’s degree in another field with at least 18 hours of master’s level work for the field in which they will teach.  For the graduate level courses, an individual must have a doctorate in the appropriate teaching field.  All forms requested by the SPCS and the University’s Human Resources Office as well as official transcripts are required prior to a contract being issued. Adjunct faculty will be expected to meet classes as scheduled, to strive to improve teaching effectiveness, to take a sympathetic interest in the progress and development of each student, to keep accurate records of the academic standing of each student, and to hand in promptly all reports of grades and other information required by the dean, the registrar, the provost, or the president.   Adjunct faculty are expected to attend the two adjunct faculty meetings, SPCS Night (held in May of each year) as well as the SPCS Commencement Ceremony. If you are interested in other employment opportunities at the University of Richmond, please revisit our Careers site at   http://jobs.richmond.edu . The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered, focused on preparing students “to live lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a global and pluralistic society.” The University of Richmond is committed to developing a diverse workforce and student body, and to modeling an inclusive campus community which values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our university community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty---School-of-Arts---Sciences_JR100013": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - School of Arts & Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-01-03\nPart time\n\nThis posting is set up to receive applications for Adjunct Faculty already identified by University of Richmond’s School of Arts & Sciences. We are not currently recruiting for these roles and you should  only apply if you have been specifically instructed to  by either University of Richmond’s Human Resource Department or the School of Arts & Sciences. Adjunct faculty members in the School of Arts & Sciences teach courses that draw upon their rich variety of academic disciplines and work experiences. All part-time faculty must meet SACSCOC regulation requirements.  They must have earned either a masters degree in their respective teaching field or a masters degree in another field with at least 18 hours of masters level work for the field in which they will teach. If an individual that does not meet these requirements, but has experience and skills that could be justified to SACSCOC, a justification letter can be provided to support these qualifications.  Adjunct faculty will be expected to meet classes as scheduled, to strive to improve teaching effectiveness, to take a sympathetic interest in the progress and development of each student, to keep accurate records of the academic standing of each student, and to hand in promptly all reports of grades and other information required by the deans, the Registrar, the Provost, or the President. If you are interested in other employment opportunities at the University of Richmond please revisit our Careers site at  http://jobs.richmond.edu . The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered, focused on preparing students “to live lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a global and pluralistic society.” The University of Richmond is committed to developing a diverse workforce and student body, and to modeling an inclusive campus community which values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our university community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Legal-Writing-Faculty--Open-Rank-_JR101509": {
      "title": "Legal Writing Faculty (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nThe University of Richmond School of Law is seeking applicants for two full-time faculty members to join our first-year Legal Analysis and Writing program in the 2027-2028 school year.  Both experienced and entry-level candidates are encouraged to apply.  Appointments may be made at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or full Professor of Law, Legal Practice, commensurate with the candidate’s qualifications and experience. The University of Richmond’s first-year Legal Analysis and Writing ( LAW) course is a two-semester, five-credit program (three credits in the Fall and two in the Spring) that focuses on legal analysis and core principles of communication and expository writing, such as audience and purpose analysis, organization, and presentation.  In addition to the LAW course, first-year students take a one-credit legal research course taught by library faculty. Faculty members will teach one section of LAW for first-year J.D. students.  Sections typically contain between 27 and 30 students.  Faculty will also teach one additional course in line with their experience, expertise, and school need. The five-member LAW faculty team coordinates learning objectives to ensure all first-year students receive instruction in the same fundamental legal writing and analysis skills.  All faculty members are committed to providing multiple opportunities for students to practice written and oral communication and significant personalized feedback on all major assignments.  Faculty members have autonomy with respect to all pedagogical choices for achieving these objectives, including reading assignments, classroom exercises, teaching materials, and problem design, selection, and implementation. Minimum Qualifications: J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school Excellent written and oral communication, research, and analytic skills Preferred Qualifications: At least three years of legal practice and/or legal education teaching experience Experience effectively teaching first-year J.D. legal analysis and writing courses Experience using or teaching generative AI platforms and other legal technology Experience with legal writing course design, problem design, and legal pedagogy Experience mentoring, coaching, or training new attorneys, legal interns, or other junior colleagues, with particular emphasis on written and oral assignment feedback Experience using inclusive pedagogy to support students with varied backgrounds and levels of preparation Experience working in a collaborative, team-based environment Involvement with legal writing, legal education, or legal professional organizations The faculty position is full-time, ten-month, and in-person. Faculty at all ranks are awarded voting rights (for all but tenure hiring and promotion) and professional development funds.  Legal writing faculty are eligible for summer writing grants and research assistant support, though scholarship is not a mandatory component of the legal writing faculty position.  Faculty will serve on faculty committees and become an integral part of the faculty-governance structure. For more information on the School of Law, please visit https://law.richmond.edu . Applicants should apply online at  https://jobs.richmond.edu  and submit a CV and cover letter.  The cover letter should include information about the candidate’s teaching experience and pedagogical philosophy, or their mentoring philosophies and approaches to effective feedback. Candidates for these positions may be asked, at a later date, to provide the names and contact information for three references and copies of prior teaching evaluations, if applicable.  Review of applications will commence on October 2, 2026, and continue until the positions are filled.  The anticipated salary range for these positions is $110,000-$140,000. Salary and rank will be commensurate with background, qualifications, and experience. The University of Richmond School of Law is a top-ranked law school that combines a rigorous academic program with extensive clinical and experiential learning opportunities to prepare students for 21st-century legal practice. The Law School also cultivates a genuinely collegial community marked by engagement, civility, inclusivity, and concern for others and the greater good.  The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/UR-Spider-Management-Intern_JR101506": {
      "title": "UR Spider Management Investment Intern",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nPart time\n\nJob Description Spider Management Company has two summer 2027 Investment internship opportunities POSITION SUMMARY: Spider Management’s Investment internship program is intended to expose sophomores and juniors at the University of Richmond to a wide range of strategies across the asset management industry. Spider Management Company (“Spider Management”) is the investment office for the University of Richmond and a select group of other non-profit organizations.  Spider Management’s 23-person team is split between Investments, Operations, and Investor Relations. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES : This internship will provide valuable hands-on experience across the organization’s three teams.  Projects could include, but are not limited to, research, data collection and analysis on potential investments, report production, database management, compliance, and market analysis.  Candidates will attend team meetings, as well as have the opportunity to attend meetings with external investment managers across asset classes (meetings may be virtual or in-person).  Candidates will also have the opportunity to take on ad-hoc projects to improve the team’s processes and/or explore select topics of interest for the purpose of presenting their findings to the team. Our intern program offers the following for a Sophomore and Junior: The opportunity to immerse yourself in the daily responsibilities, projects, and initiatives Collaborate with seasoned professionals Interact with team members and senior leadership Gain exposure to various areas of the business Contribute to evaluations of prospective investment opportunities  What You Will Learn/Gain During the Internship: Basics of endowment and foundation investing Interact with and learn from world-class investment managers Fundamentals of asset allocation and portfolio management Introduction to multiple traditional and alternative investment asset classes Exposure to investment opportunities in a wide variety of sectors and geographies QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED: Indicate in cover letter if you are applying for the Sophomore Internship or the Junior Internship For Sophomores: preferred majors or minors in Business/Finance or another quantitative discipline For Juniors: REQUIRED majors or minors in Business/Finance or another quantitative discipline Must have excellent academic credentials Fully developed quantitative and analytical skills Proficiency in Microsoft Office products; advanced Excel and/or PowerPoint skills preferred Strong communication skills, both written and oral Ability to multi-task and work independently Detail-oriented and extremely proactive Self-starter High level of professionalism and integrity Cooperative attitude – must be a team player Interest in investment management industry, particularly non-profit investment management Must adhere to Spider Management Company’s Code of Ethics policy LOCATION:  In-person (Richmond, VA) CONTACTS: Spider Management staff External investment managers WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Environment – Office Essential Physical requirements – standing, sitting, typing REQUIRED: Required: current Fall 2026 Sophomore or Fall 2026 Junior Resume and cover letter Please indicate in your cover letter and resume if you are applying for the Sophomore or the Junior Internship Copy of Unofficial College Transcript Schedule : Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; schedule times may vary. Spider Management Pay Rate: $20 to $30 an hour. This is an 8 to 10-week part-time, temporary position, mid-May to mid-August 2027."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Lead-Cook_JR101496": {
      "title": "Lead Cook",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: The Lead Cook is a member of Dining Service’s food production team and ensures that all meals are produced in a timely manner according to specified recipes. Lead Cook assists the Sous Chef in the planning and execution of daily food production, and directs, assigns work to, and supervises the kitchen production staff in the absence of the Sous Chef. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: This position is designated as essential personnel and, during inclement weather and emergency situations, is required to be available to the University as needed. This position requires long periods of standing and walking, and the ability to lift 50 lbs., in conditions that are often, hot, humid, or cold. Must be able to safely use and operate typical kitchen tools and equipment. Must be able to work outdoors in varying weather conditions, on occasion. QUALIFICATIONS: Ability to lead/manage others Intermediate knowledge of culinary techniques and practices. Proficient in English written and verbal communication skills Strong verbal communication and listening skills Possesses flexibility to work in a fast paced, dynamic environment Friendly and service-oriented Demonstrates a high commitment to quality Knowledge of culinary techniques and practices Knowledge of and ability to exercise creativity in food presentation, idea generation, and menu planning Knowledge of supplies, equipment, and/or services ordering and inventory control Skilled in the planning, production, and presentation of high quality food for meals and receptions for more than 1,000 guests Knowledge of safe food handling practices and HACCP procedures Ability to use computerized food production software EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE :  High school diploma or GED. Associate degree in the culinary arts field preferred but not required. 3 years’ experience in high volume production in hotels, restaurants or college foodservice required. Safe food handler certification preferred, or certification obtained within one year of hire. (This is dependent on the assigned unit.) SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 3 (Hiring Range $19.25 to $21.50 per hour) WORK HOURS: Full Time, Non-Exempt Sunday - Saturday, Hours vary, rotating weekends"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Plumber_JR101504": {
      "title": "Plumber",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nJob Description Job Summary: Responsible to maintain all plumbing systems and their related piping and fixtures and any other duties necessary to supply sanitary and safe water to, and waste from, all the buildings of the University of Richmond. Job Duties/Responsibilities: Maintain and effect repairs on varied plumbing systems including domestic water supply, site utility and fire main supply, building and site sanitary, fixtures and specialties, storm drains. Install or modify systems for small renovation projects. Work safely in varied conditions including crawl spaces, occupied spaces, excavations, and construction areas. Ability to carry out project or maintenance work with minimal supervision. Assist in special events as required to ensure successful outcome. All Facilities employees are expected to exercise behavior in accordance with university policies as well as with the Facilities Mission and statement of Core Values. All Facilities employees are expected to comply to safety standards and demonstrate safe work habits. Subject to being on call for emergency service after normal hours on rotating basis with other tradesmen in shops. Qualifications: Knowledge, skills & ability:  Working knowledge of the International Plumbing Code. Ability to read and understand schematic diagrams. Possess basic estimating skills to accurately assess labor and material costs for projects. Working knowledge of OSHA regulations pertaining to work performed. Ability to use International Plumbing Code and perform work in accordance with IPC. Ability to use tools and equipment correctly and safely. Exercise behavior in accordance with University and Facilities guidelines, policies, procedures, mission and shared values. Always display professional manners and appearance. Ability to troubleshoot and repair problems. Communicate clearly and professionally to all necessary key interfaces information pertaining to quality completion of work. Physically capable of working as an electrician, including work from ladders and high platforms or lifts. Ability to use specialty and heavy equipment. Soldering and/or welding ability.  Required: Must have a valid Driver's License and be able to produce a driving record with a minimum of 3 years of driving history in good standing Education & experience:  Technical training certificate in Plumbing Journeyman’s Certificate 5 years’ progressive experience in plumbing construction and/or maintenance. Work Hours: Full-time; Non-exempt Monday-Friday 7:30AM-4:00PM, however, flexibility is required. All Facilities employees are designated as essential personnel and are subject to work outside the normal work schedule on an emergency basis and are subject to planned overtime work as required by their immediate supervisor. Pay Structure: Grade 5 Hourly ($21.09 to $27.95 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Academic-Advisor_JR101525": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nPart time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY:  Please submit a resume and cover letter The Academic Advisor-Athletics position will provide a small number of student-athletes with critical writing support, while working to improve their reading and vocabulary skills so as to make them more effective college-level writers.  This position will also oversee those students in various required study hall sessions. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  Review all academic objectives of the assigned students prior to each study sessions. (25%) Teach and promote study skills, including but not limited to note-taking, reading comprehension, and test-taking strategies. (25%) Teach and promote time management skills. (25%) Assist in referring assigned students to the appropriate support personnel. (20%) Provide weekly reports to the Assistant Athletic Director for Academics or to student-athlete’s assigned athletic advisor. (5%)  CONTACTS :   This position will require consistent communication with the Assistant Athletic Director for Academics and the student-athlete’s assigned athletic advisor to discuss the latter’s academic progress.  This position will require, at times, communication with appropriate coaches and support staff, as coordinated by the Assistant Athletic Director. SUPERVISION EXERCISED :   Supervise student-athletes on a consistent basis. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Office environment (sitting, standing, walking, typing) Occasional nights & weekend work QUALIFICATIONS: Must possess excellent writing, communication and editorial skills. Knowledge of basic college-level writing as well as an understanding about the nature of writing for an academic audience is required. Ability to maintain a professional and positive teacher/student relationship. Must be able to assess the academic preparedness and writing skills of the assigned students and then enact an academic enhancement plan. Adherence to all NCAA, Atlantic-10 and University rules and regulations is required. EDUCATION: Bachelor’s degree required, master’s degree preferred in a liberal arts discipline. SCHEDULE:    Hourly, less than 20 hours per week.  Monday through Friday, some evenings and weekends.  Pay Range:    $15.00 to $22 per hour, non-exempt"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Associate-Sr-Associate-Athletic-Trainer---Men-s-Basketball_JR101500-1": {
      "title": "Associate/Sr Associate Athletic Trainer - Men's Basketball",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: Associate/Sr Associate Athletic Trainer - Men's Basketball Assumes overall responsibility for the application, administration, and documentation of comprehensive healthcare services for high-risk and/or high-profile Division I teams and student-athletes as assigned by the Associate Athletic Director of Sports Medicine and Student-Athlete Welfare.  Provides counseling and health education to student-athletes and coaches.  Manages emergency response to emergency situations that occur within the athletic department.  Collaborates and works closely with all members of athletic department and in particular as division with other members of the SP4 team. Supervision of 1-3 Assistant and/or Associate Athletic Trainers. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Clinical application and administration of comprehensive health care services for the assigned high-risk and/or high-profile sports programs (70%) Onsite care and management of injuries and illnesses including prevention, evaluation, management, and rehabilitation. Assess risk of injury, perform ergonomic evaluation, self-care implementation, and overall wellness promotion. Electronic documentation of all patient encounters. Coordinate and implement injury prevention programs and customized preventative strategies and programs to each patient. Minimize risk of injury through awareness, education, and prevention strategies. Provide accommodation recommendations related to sport, academics, and activities of daily living for patients with restrictions or limitations. Carry out medical care for assigned patients per the University of Richmond standard operating policies and procedures. Communicate with Team Physicians, Coaching Staff, Strength and Conditioning Staff, and other essential personal both written and verbal reports on the health status of each student-athletes. Collaborate as part of the SP4 team on student-athlete data analytics and performance. Serves as a senior leader within Sports Medicine with responsibility for staff supervision, operational oversight, and departmental leadership initiatives. (30%) Recruit, hire, train, and supervise/mentor assigned Assistant and/or Associate Athletic Trainers.  Ensure Provide oversight of various sports medicine facilities and administrative areas, which may include emergency action planning, risk management, coordination with team physicians, and policy development/improvement. CONTACTS : Leadership and Development – to collaborate as part of SP4 team Compliance – To work in cooperation on medically related compliance matters Academics – To communicate the effect medical status on academic attendance or performance. Facilities and Events – To communicate emergency action plans and severe weather updates. Athletic Equipment Manager – To collaborate on protective equipment and staff clothing orders Athletic PR – to provide communication to media about medical issues and update sports medicine website. Student Health Center – Referral and assessment of student-athletes. Disability Services – to coordinate disability services for student-athletes Counseling and Psychological Services – Referral for student-athletes with mental health needs. Strength & Conditioning – Supervision of health and safety and communication of injured student-athlete limitations. Campus Police – to collaborate on emergency response. Physical Plant – To conduct facility maintenance and repair. Custodial Services – To collaborate on sanitation of medical facilities. Registered Dietitians – To provide education and counseling to student-athletes. Team Physicians and Consulting Subspecialists – To provide medical services to student-athletes. Resource Exchange Center – To provide nutritional supplements and drug education. SUPERVISION EXERCISED : Supervision of 1-3 Assistant or Associate Athletic Trainers. May supervise athletic training students and work-study aides WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: Environment – Requires the employee to work both inside and outside in heat/cold, wet/humid, and dry/arid conditions. Exposures encountered - Frequently required to use personal protective equipment to prevent exposure to hazardous materials. Essential physical requirements: Climbing, standing, stooping, typing, running Physical effort/lifting: Heavy Lifting – over 50 pounds. Required to work early mornings, late nights, weekends, travel, and be on-call as a regular part of the job.  QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge and clinical skill in the prevention, recognition, evaluation, and treatment of athletic injuries; acute care and emergency response to athletic injuries; rehabilitation and reconditioning of athletic injuries; and health care administration A thorough understanding of evidence-based medicine and appropriate current standards of care in Sport Medicine. A thorough knowledge in anatomy, physiology, kinesiology, exercise physiology, nutrition, and strength and conditioning principles Strong verbal and written communication skills and interpersonal communication skills. Adherence to all applicable NCAA, conference, and institutional rules and regulations is required. Applied Knowledge: Athletic trainers must have knowledge of a large range of medical problems. Assessment, evaluation skills and working with other healthcare prof essionals will be required. Decision-Making Skills: Ability to think critically and make independent decisions regarding the assessment, treatment, rehabilitation, and return-to-play decisions per state licensure guidelines. Attention to Detail: Athletic trainers should be thorough and detailed with injury tracking and documentation. Interpersonal Skills: Ability to communicate effectively in high stress/pressure situation when talking to patients, medical professionals, staff and families. Must have the ability to organize, prioritize and perform multiple tasks with little or no supervision. Education & experience :  Minimum of 5 years of collegiate or professional athletic training experience required BOC Certified Athletic Trainer Master’s degree is required CPR / AED Certified for Professional Rescuer VA Board of Medicine License to practice Athletic Training NPI Number is required Requires a valid driver's license for at least 3 years Schedule: Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm. 38.75 hours per week. Some nights and weekends. Requires travel. Salary Range: $50,461 to $76,877; salary based on experience level"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Banquet-Captain_JR101521": {
      "title": "Banquet Captain",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY : As a member of the University Catering team is responsible for managing and executing the set-up and break-down of all assigned catered events while exhibiting professionalism, flexibility, and grace under pressure as the representative of University Dining at a catering event. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge of and ability to exercise basic management principles, including leading and training staff, and ability to work cooperatively as a part of a team Skilled in using effective time management, problem solving, and public relations techniques Ability to understand, follow written and interact given verbal instructions and procedures Ability to exercise flexibility, work independently without direct supervision, and work under pressure to meet deadlines in a fast paced environment with frequent interruptions Ability to provide a high level of customer service Ability to monitor and/or maintain quality control standards Basic knowledge of safe food handling practices Knowledge of culinary skills and techniques and ability to train and lead others Ability to use computers and learn new software EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE :  H.S. Diploma or GED required 2 to 4 years of banquet experience required 2-4 years supervisor experience preferred WORK HOURS : Full Time, Non-exempt 8 hours daily/40 hours weekly Sunday - Saturday, hours vary (AM and PM hours), weekends vary SALARY: Pay grade 3 ($19.25/hour to $22.50/hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Rowland-Bicentennial-Fellow-in-Health-Studies_JR101529-1": {
      "title": "Rowland Bicentennial Fellow in Health Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nThe University of Richmond invites applications for the full-time Rowland Bicentennial Fellow in Health Studies at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor to begin in August 2027.   The Department of Health Studies (HS) ( https://hs.richmond.edu/ ) equips students with theoretical and empirical frameworks for understanding the social, cultural, environmental, biological, ethical, economic, legal, and policy contexts that shape individual and population health. HS alumni work in medicine and other clinical professions, public health, healthcare policy and management, and many other health-related fields. Under Richmond’s teacher-scholar model, professors typically teach undergraduate courses, author peer-reviewed scholarship, mentor student researchers, and participate in departmental and university governance. We seek a faculty colleague who shares our commitment to student-centered undergraduate education with service expectations tailored to career trajectory. The department welcomes applicants with sufficient training and expertise to teach Health Systems & Policy plus one of the department’s two other introductory core courses in global (public) health or epidemiology & health research methods. Candidates with demonstrated strength in applied data analysis relevant to population health are particularly encouraged to apply. We welcome a range of methodological expertise, including quantitative, computational, and mixed-methods approaches to analyzing health data. A doctoral degree in public health or a doctoral degree in a closely related field with additional public health training (e.g., an MPH) must be completed prior to the start date.   Applicants should apply online at http://jobs.richmond.edu and upload a CV, a brief cover letter, a 500-word teaching statement, and a 500-word research statement. The teaching statement should (a) outline their training and expertise to teach Health Systems & Policy , (b) name the additional core HS courses they could teach and any elective HS courses the applicant is interested in teaching and/or developing, (c) detail their training and proficiency in applied data analysis , (d) describe any teaching experience or training, (e) articulate the applicant’s teaching philosophy and strategies for teaching effectiveness, and (f) express the applicant's involvement in and commitment to inclusive pedagogy. The research statement should describe the applicant’s research area(s) and plans, including plans for meaningfully involving undergraduate students in research, and the nature of the applied data analysis in their research. Applicants will also be asked to provide the names and contact information for three references who may be asked, later, to submit letters of recommendation.   Review of applications will commence on October 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Please address any questions about the position to department chair Dr. Rick Mayes ( bmayes@richmond.edu ).   The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $85,000. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Teaching-Faculty-of-Latin-American--Latino---Iberian-Studies_JR101530": {
      "title": "Teaching Faculty of Latin American, Latino & Iberian Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Latin American, Latino & Iberian Studies in the School of Arts & Sciences at the University of Richmond invites applications for a teaching faculty member to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. This is a full-time, non-tenure-track, continuing position with benefits.   The candidate will teach 6 units per year, whereby 2 units often constitute a single intensive class that meets five times per week. The department ’s interdisciplinary curriculum highlights the cultural and linguistic diversity of the Spanish-speaking world and is committed to excellence in undergraduate teaching at all levels of the curriculum. The beginning and intermediate language courses that this candidate will teach develop students’ linguistic and intercultural competence, thereby fulfilling an important requirement of our General Education curriculum, the Web of Inquiry , and prepare students for more advanced courses in support of the University’s mission . While not required, the candidate will also have the occasional opportunity to teach more advanced Spanish courses with specific thematic foci (e.g. politics, media, literature), first year seminars in English, and the intensive intermediate Spanish courses taught abroad or on campus during the summer.   In addition to teaching, the candidate will co-direct the Spanish language program and serve on committees as needed. The candidate will work closely with a fellow co-director of the Spanish language program. Their shared duties include overseeing curricular development of beginning and intermediate Spanish courses, which are taught by full-time faculty members; advising students; and supervising the undergraduate assistant teachers who lead weekly practice sessions to build oral fluency. The candidate should bring creative and data-driven ideas and the administrative skills required to continually improve the language program. As both co-director and as language teacher, the candidate should have the knowledge and agility to respond thoughtfully to emerging technologies, including generative AI, as well as a commitment to improving students’ ability to engage in uniquely human communication. The candidate will also be expected to build community and encourage belonging by connecting students from a wide range of linguistic and cultural backgrounds to opportunities across campus. There is no obligation to do research, but there are opportunities to do so.   Candidates must hold a Master’s or doctoral degree in a relevant field such as Spanish, language program direction, second-language acquisition, or applied linguistics by the time of appointment. The candidate should have a record of excellence in teaching beginning and intermediate Spanish language courses at the college level. The ideal candidate will have experience in language program direction as well.   Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.richmond.edu and submit the following five documents: a one-page (single-spaced) cover letter; a curriculum vitae; a one-page (single-spaced) teaching statement; a one-page (single-spaced) program direction statement; and a single document, not to exceed five pages, containing evidence of teaching effectiveness. The teaching statement should describe the candidate’s teaching philosophy and methods, including their approach to teaching a diverse student body, and provide context for the evidence of teaching effectiveness submitted. That evidence of teaching effectiveness could take the form of a syllabus, lesson plan, activity, assessment, or other material. The program direction statement should outline the candidate’s experience and preparation for language program administration as well as their general approach to and vision for directing the Spanish language program at the University of Richmond.   Candidates for this position may be asked, at a later date, to provide a more comprehensive teaching portfolio, student evaluations, and the names and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Review of applications will commence October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Elizabeth Kissling, department chair, with any questions. For more information on the department/school and its programs, please visit https://lais.richmond.edu/.   The starting salary for the position is budgeted at $77.000. Compensation is determined by the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, and skills. The University of Richmond is a private university located just a short drive from downtown Richmond, Virginia. Through its five schools and wide array of campus programming, the University combines the best qualities of a small liberal arts college and a large university. With approximately 4,000 students, an 8:1 student-faculty ratio, and more than 90% of traditional undergraduate students living on campus, the University is remarkably student-centered. The mission of the University of Richmond is to educate in an academically challenging, intellectually vibrant, and collaborative community dedicated to the holistic development of students and the production of scholarly and creative work. A Richmond education prepares students for lives of purpose, thoughtful inquiry, and responsible leadership in a diverse world. The University of Richmond’s strategic priorities are academic excellence, belonging and community, access and affordability, well-being, and experiential learning and community engagement. The University seeks to model a relentlessly welcoming and inclusive campus community that values the expression of difference in ways that promote excellence in teaching, learning, personal development, and institutional success. Our academic community strongly encourages applications that are in keeping with this commitment."
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Special-Events-Coordinator_JR101518": {
      "title": "Special Events Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY: Plans and execute special event activities for the Robins School of Business. To include educational events, conferences, student events, networking events and other selected events involving faculty, staff, students, alumni and the business community. Ensure smooth-running, effective events. RESPONSIBILITIES:   Special Event Coordinator job responsibilities include but are not limited to:   Event Planning/Management   Work with supervisor in the business school to develop special event schedule for the academic year. Ensure that all managed events align with school’s mission and goals and are within budgetary limits. Responsible for coordination and execution of assigned special events and/or programs which includes: development of invitation lists, multi-channel marketing and promotions, online registration, event space procurement, monetary deposits/returns, catering, lodging, technology, program materials, and on-site management. Makes logistical recommendations based on the needs of the special event and develops event timeline for each assigned event. Serves as principal liaison between contractors, vendors, organizers, speakers, faculty, and administrators with regards to all facets of logistical operations supporting assigned programs and events. Database Management   Assist with the management and updating of database systems with regards to recording data on special events. Robins School of Business – Undergraduate Student Organization Support   Serve as liaison with student organizations based in the Business School as it pertains to events. Collaborate with Director of the Center Professional Skills and Development and students on their event programs and assist with navigating campus platforms, policies and procedures as it pertains to special events. Maintain student organization access to university systems. QUALIFICATIONS ·   General knowledge and understanding of meeting planning and event management principles to include managing event budgets. ·   Collaborative team player with a high degree of initiative, problem-solving skills and ability to work independently. ·   Thorough knowledge of Microsoft Office and experience with systems for data management, space reservation, registration, online event programs. ·   Strong organizational skills are required to balance a variety of complex tasks with a high degree of competency and accuracy; ability to manage multiple events and be deadline oriented. ·   Strong oral and written communication skills. Ability to work with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. ·   Ability to work well under pressure and handle last minute projects and shifts in priorities.   EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE ·   Bachelor's degree or an equivalent combination of education and experience ·   2+ years of special event experience (planning, managing and executing multiple complex events). ·   Higher Education experience a plus.   WORK HOURS: ·   Full-time, non-exempt position ·   Typical schedule is 8:30am to 5:00pm, Monday through Friday (38.75 hours per week). Some morning, evening and weekend hours required for assigned special events. Schedule will be flexed to accommodate special event hours.   SALARY STRUCTURE: Pay Grade 5 (Hiring Range $21.09 to $27.95 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/UR-Floater_JR100938": {
      "title": "UR Floater",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nJob Description Summary The Administrative Floater is a casual, as-needed position. The role covers long-term assignments, vacations, or special projects. This position provides administrative support as the primary contact in various offices and departments across campus. Position supports department leaders by providing smooth office or project management, requiring professionalism, confidentiality, flexibility, and the ability to work independently. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:    Administrative: Provides administrative and secretarial support, which may include typing correspondence, letters, speeches, presentations, and other relevant documents.  Schedules appointments and maintains calendars, makes travel arrangements, screens and handles telephone and email communications, greets and directs visitors, and deals with administrative inquiries as appropriate.  Updates data in appropriate systems and may update department website.  Prepares various charts and reports. Functions/Events: Plans, schedules, and coordinates logistics for social/business functions (breakfasts, luncheons, dinners), which may include reservations, catering and accommodations for overnight guests. Budgets/Expenses: Monitors and reconciles credit card statements and budgets; submits invoices and reimbursement requests for payment internally and externally.  Miscellaneous : Orders supplies for the office, maintains office files, and requests service for office equipment.  Keeps current on the latest campus procedures and technologies through meetings and training sessions offered by the University.  Assists with special projects as assigned. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Does not provide supervision to other individuals. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT:   Office environment; some outside work may be required QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, skills & ability :  Ability to work independently, multitask, and meet deadlines using discretion in confidential matters. Strong interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to work effectively with a diverse range of individuals. Proficient use of Microsoft Word, Outlook, Excel, Publisher, and PowerPoint. Knowledge of supplies, equipment, and/or services ordering and inventory control. Ability to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. Ability to gather data, compile information, and prepare reports. Records maintenance skills. Ability to maintain calendars and schedule appointments. Database management skills. Ability to analyze and solve problems. Ability to make administrative/procedural decisions and judgments. Organizing and coordinating skills. Ability to record and transcribe meeting minutes. Receptionist skills Education & experience : High school diploma or GED required and some college preferred. 3-5 years of administrative support experience in an office setting. Schedule Varies. Less than 15 hours per week. Salary may vary based on position, experience, project, and required skills. Grade 1: $15.00 - $18.00 per hour"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Operations-Business-Analyst_JR101538": {
      "title": "Operations Business Analyst (On-Site)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJob Description Please note that this position will REQUIRE you to work on the university campus and a fully remote schedule is not available. The Campus Operations Business Analyst collects, analyzes, and reports data that measures institutional performance across sustainability, facilities, dining, campus business services, finance, and operations. The Analyst will manage and interpret datasets, coordinate assessments and reports, and collaborate with campus partners to enhance transparency and support data-driven decision-making. Working with Campus Operations staff, the Analyst will help track and monitor building systems and equipment, utility performance, sustainability initiatives, food costs, and other financial and operational metrics. This role plays a key part in advancing the University’s operational goals and reports to the Office of Campus Operations, working closely with department leaders.   Job Duties/Responsibilities: Data Analytics, Performance & Reporting Design, implement, and maintain data collection processes for sustainability, facilities, dining, campus business services, utilities, and other financial and operations metrics. Aggregate and validate data from multiple sources (building management systems, utility meters, financial systems, POS, surveys, spreadsheets). Maintain data integrity, document data definitions, methodologies, data sources and procedures, and manage central data repositories. Develop, calculate, and maintain key performance indicators (KPIs) and benchmarks for energy, water, waste, equipment uptime, food cost, labor productivity, revenue, and other operational metrics. Perform trend, root-cause and variance analysis to explain performance changes and identify improvement opportunities. Conduct scenario and cost-benefit analyses to support operational decisions and capital planning. Produce regular and ad hoc reports, executive summaries, interactive dashboards, visualizations, and scorecards using BI tools (e.g., Power BI, Tableau) to communicate performance, findings, and actionable recommendations to leadership and stakeholders. Operational Analysis & Assessment Support ongoing tracking of building systems and equipment condition, maintenance schedules, and utility performance metrics. Work with maintenance and engineering teams to configure and validate Building Management Systems (BMS) and meter data for analytics. Coordinate assessments, surveys and program evaluations (e.g., energy audits, waste audits, dining cost reviews). Coordinate the completion of sustainability assessments and reports, including regular submissions to AASHE STARS, Sustainable Campus Index, APPA, and state reporting, while performing gap analyses to identify areas for enhancement and supporting alignment with institutional and regulatory reporting requirements. Support initiatives to improve operational efficiency and sustainability (energy conservation measures, waste reduction, LEED certification). Help prepare materials for accreditation, regulatory submissions, or external benchmarking programs. Program & Project Support Participate in cross-functional working groups and project teams; act as a liaison between Campus Operations and other units. Assist with budgeting, forecasting, and financial reconciliation related to operations as required. Recommend process improvements, data-driven policies and best practices to increase transparency and operational effectiveness. Collaboration, Training & Data Support Present findings and recommendations to leadership, staff, and campus partners and respond to data requests from internal and external stakeholders in a timely manner. Train departmental staff on data entry processes, reporting tools, and interpretation of KPIs. Build and maintain relationships across campus departments to ensure efficient, consistent, and integrated data collection processes and provide analytical and technical support on data-related projects to team members, committees, and working groups.   Working Conditions/Physical Effort · Office-based work environment · Must adhere to department and University safety standards and promote safe work practices   Qualifications: Knowledge, skills & ability:  Demonstrated proficiency in quantitative analysis and advanced use of Microsoft Excel; familiarity with EnergyCAP, SIMAP, Tableau, ArcGIS, and other data management or visualization tools preferred. Demonstrated ability to aggregate, validate, and maintain data from multiple systems (BMS, utility meters, financial systems, POS, spreadsheets). Familiarity with key operational and sustainability metrics (energy, water, waste, equipment uptime, food cost, labor productivity, transportation, fleet, revenue). Ability to perform trend, variance, root-cause, and scenario/cost-benefit analyses and translate findings into recommendations. Excellent written and verbal communication skills; ability to prepare clear narratives, executive summaries, and presentations for diverse stakeholders. Experience coordinating cross-functional projects and working collaboratively with operational and technical teams. Strong attention to detail, organization, and documentation practices for data integrity and reproducibility. Understanding of/or ability to learn building management systems, operations, and controls. LEED Accredited Professional (O+M or NC) credential preferred, or willingness to obtain within one year of hire. Certified Energy Manager (CEM) credentials are valued   Education & experience :  Bachelor’s degree in business Analytics, Data Science, Economics, Finance, Environmental Science, Sustainability, Engineering, Statistics, or related field preferred; equivalent relevant professional experience, training, or certifications will also be considered. 2–4 years of professional experience in data analysis, operations analytics, facilities management analytics, sustainability reporting, or similar roles. Work Hours: Full-time; Exempt position Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM This position requires you to work on campus Salary Structure: Grade 7 Salaried ($58,011 to $86,299 annually)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Member-Services-Assistant_JR100601": {
      "title": "Member Services Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nJob Description The University of Richmond Weinstein Recreation Center is Hiring! JOB SUMMARY: Assistants are responsible for Weinstein Center Member Services operations during assigned shifts. Primary responsibilities include providing excellent customer service and information to all Weinstein Center members.  Provide locker assignments, distribute and collect informal recreation equipment, and register members for programs, classes, services, or trips.  Enroll members in the Fusion software system.    SPECIFIC DUTIES:  Greets all visitors and members. Directs individuals as needed.  Provides current program and schedule information to members by phone and in person. Answers questions and distributes printed materials as needed.  Assist members in creating account access to the FusionGo app and member portal.  Ensures eligibility policies are enforced. Enrolls members in the Fusion system.  Utilizing the Fusion software, enrolls members in various programs or services  Provides accurate membership information to prospective members  Distributes informal recreation equipment following all check-out procedures  Documents and stores all Lost & Found items  Documents and assigns lockers and locks for all locker rooms inside the Weinstein Center  Responsible for daily racquetball and squash court reservations.  Processed all guest fee transactions, including cash and check payments  Maintains a clean and organized work environment  Communicates with the On-duty Facility Manager as needed  Serves as a first responder in the event of an emergency in the Weinstein Center  Attends all scheduled training and monthly meetings. Follows all emergency procedures in compliance with the risk management plan. Complies with all Recreation employee guidelines.   Provides a welcoming and inclusive environment for all patrons in Health & Well-being facilities, programs, and communications while providing a commitment to work effectively with students, faculty, and staff from diverse backgrounds    QUALIFICATIONS:   Excellent customer service skills Prior experience with cash handling is preferred  Ability to read, interpret, and enforce policies and procedures  Dependable and able to work a flexible schedule  Current CPR/AED certification required within 30 days of employment  SCHEDULE: Varies, less than 20 hours per week SALARY: $15.00 per hour"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Cook-I_JR101542-2": {
      "title": "Cook I",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nJob Description JOB SUMMARY: As an entry level kitchen position working under direction, this position prepares food in quantity and is responsible for maintaining high standards for food quality while practicing proper sanitation, food safety, and cooking procedures. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIE: Operations : (90%) • Cooks and prepares food as directed according to established recipes, special dietary or nutritional restrictions, or numbers of portions. Cooking may include display cooking • Cleans, cuts, and cooks all food products as assigned, including meat, fish, poultry, starches, and vegetables • Garnishes foods • Cleans, sets up, and breaks down kitchen equipment, appliances, and work areas to ensure cleanliness and functional operation • Operates kitchen, line equipment and machinery according to established safety procedures • Covers, labels, and dates all food products, and follows all food safety rules for proper storage • Monitors serving lines and ensures an adequate supply of food items, per the menu of the day, for the entire service period • Monitors and records temperatures of foods stored in refrigeration, in hot boxes, and on the serving line to ensure safe temperatures • Performs other duties, as assigned Additional Duties : (10%) • Maintains cleanliness of the production's areas • Washes pots, pans, dishes, utensils, and other cooking equipment, as needed CONTACTS : Contacts are primarily with direct supervisor and others in group or department to give and receive information. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT : Food services QUALIFICATIONS : Knowledge, skills & ability : • Ability to simultaneously handle multiple priorities • Proficient in English written and verbal communication skills • Possesses flexibility to work in a fast paced, dynamic environment • Friendly and service-oriented • Demonstrates a high commitment to quality • Excellent collaboration and team building skills • Basic knowledge of culinary practices and techniques • Knowledge of quantity food production principles and safe food handling practices • Safe food handler certification preferred, or certification obtained within one year of hire, required. Required dependent upon assigned unit Education & experience : • H.S. Diploma or GED required • One year of related work experience preferred Work Schedule: •Full-time; Nonexempt •11:30am-8:30pm, Sunday through Saturday 5 days/week, rotating schedule Pay Structure: Grade 1 Hourly ( $16.75 to $18.25 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Small-Engine-Equipment-Technician_JR101541": {
      "title": "Small Engine/Equipment Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nJob Description JOB SUMMARY: Perform skilled, reliable and quality services with the inspection, maintenance and repair of university vehicles and equipment, powered by gas, diesel, electric, propane, and/or hybrid. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Small Engine/Landscape Equipment Repair Repairs small engines including but not limited to pressure washers, landscaping equipment, generators and other various gasoline, diesel, and battery equipment Preforms monthly battery checks on university golf carts across campus Repair or replace engines, carburetors, fuel systems, ignition components, belts, bearings, clutches, and other components. Disassemble, inspect, clean, repair, and reassemble engines and equipment. Perform routine maintenance, including oil changes, filter replacement, spark plug service, lubrication, and adjustments on small engine equipment Notifies supervisor of any small engine or golf carts that will not pass internal inspection or will need to be replaced soon  Vehicle Maintenance & Repair Provides accurate and timely vehicle maintenance and repairs of university vehicles Perform routine maintenance, including oil changes, filter replacement, spark plug service, lubrication, and adjustments on vehicles. Notifies supervisor of any vehicles that will not pass internal inspection or will need to be replaced soon Other Duties Communicates with the vehicle/equipment customer about diagnosis and repair Inputs and works with supervisor/office admin entering new equipment into work order system Documents notes in the work order system on each vehicle or piece of equipment after work is completed to track history of service for each vehicle Maintains a clean and safe work area Other duties as assigned by supervisor   WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: All Facilities employees are designated as essential personnel and are subject to work outside the normal work schedule on an emergency basis and are also subject to planned overtime work as required by the immediate supervisor QUALIFICATIONS:      Knowledge, skills & ability :  Excellent knowledge of mechanical, electrical and electronic components of vehicles & small engines Required to provide basic mechanical hand tools to support repair and maintenance tasks per industry standard Experience using shop equipment such as lifts, tire changing equipment, alignment machines, and scan tools Working knowledge of vehicle diagnostic systems and methods Demonstrate safe work habits and comply to safety standards. Skilled and experienced in the inspections, maintenance, repair and operation of vehicles and equipment. Valid Virginia driver's license in good standing with DMV and record meets standards of the University's insurance carrier. EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: H.S. Diploma or GED required 2 years of technical training (Preferred) 3 years of experience in equipment maintenance and/or repair. WORK HOURS: Full-time; Nonexempt Monday-Friday 6:30AM-3:00PM All Facilities employees are designated as essential personnel and are subject to work outside the normal work schedule on an emergency basis and are also subject to planned overtime work as required by the immediate supervisor PAY STRUCTURE: Grade 5 Hourly ($21.09 to $27.95 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Manager-Operation-Development-and-Alumni-Relations_JR101526-1": {
      "title": "Manager Operation Development and Alumni Relations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nJob Description POSITION SUMMARY: The School of Law’s Operations Manager for Development and Alumni Relations, reporting to the Director of Annual Giving & Alumni Relations, is responsible for managing Law Advancement’s annual giving and alumni relations programming, events, and operations. As the data liaison and operations specialist on the team, the Operations Manager also produces Law Advancement reports, is skilled in fundraising software, and manages team administrative needs. The Operations Manager will be a self-starter who is forward-thinking, able to manage multiple priorities, and navigate uncertainty as new programs are built and existing are evolving and expanding.   The Manager is collaborative as he/she works in concert with other members of the Law School Advancement office, the Dean, the Law School’s administrative team (career services, student affairs, admissions, communications), and the central Advancement Division.   JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES:  Develop, manage, and implement Annual Giving & Alumni Relations programming under strategic oversight of the Director of Annual Giving & Alumni Relations. (70%): Develop, manage, and implement year-round annual giving and alumni relations communications (print, digital, phone), including participation in the University’s annual Giving Day. Manage and systematically execute gift tracking, and develop and manage subsequent acknowledgment and stewardship processes and communications. In partnership with the Law Communications & Events team, maintain primary responsibility for implementing Law Advancement events, including managing invitations and RSVPs, coordinating day-of staffing and logistics, preparing event briefing materials, and preparing and sending pre- and post-event communications. Design and implement a student philanthropy program to build a culture of philanthropy among the student body. Serve as the primary law alumni and donor data liaison and operations specialist on the team. (25%) Track and manage data in donor database (Blackbaud CRM), including but not limited to: pulling lists and reports; building queries and exports; and submitting data to University partners for database entry. Prepare and send all mass emails and event registration pages (via Blackbaud Internet Solutions) to law alumni, including as needed for external law departments. Proactively monitor and analyze performance of development and alumni relations print and electronic communications, including segmenting and tracking mailings, tracking open rates for emails, etc. Monitor and report to senior management on law school budgets, allocate transactions to account codes, and submits monthly credit card reconciliation statements. Serve as first point of contact for Law Advancement inquiries via law alumni inbox and phone. Manage team administrative needs, including but not limited to managing large print jobs, designing event materials, coordinating mailings, etc. Manage a part-time student employee who handles administrative tasks such as stuffing envelopes, sending mail, etc. Serve as a collaborative member and thought partner on the Law Advancement team (5%): Attend and be an active participant at regularly scheduled Law and University Advancement meetings and events. Be a thought partner, providing  strategic input on The Law Advancement team’s various initiatives (alumni relations, annual & leadership giving, prospect management, and donor relations) and how they may be leveraged to support both annual giving and alumni relations efforts.   QUALIFICATIONS: Ability to exercise good judgment, demonstrate an understanding of the ethical principles associated with working in development, and to use discretion in interactions with members of the UR community. Excellent interpersonal and leadership skills, along with the ability to work with diverse internal and external constituencies including donors and volunteers. Proven ability to work effectively with honesty and transparency in a collaborative, multi-level team environment with a high degree of collegiality. Strong written communication, organizational, and analytical skills. Ability to manage multiple tasks and set priorities in a fast-paced environment. Skill in the use of Microsoft Office and fundraising software applications, including database management. Must have a valid Driver's License.   EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE: Bachelor’s Degree required. Minimum of two to four years of experience managing projects and/or events is required. Requires a valid driver's license and a motor vehicle records check Experience in development or alumni relations is strongly preferred. Computer proficiency is required, and experience with Blackbaud CRM, or other donor database is strongly preferred. A high degree of personal integrity is essential. WORK HOURS : 38.75 hours per week. Monday through Friday, 8:30 am to 5 pm; occasional travel, some evenings and weeks SALARY STRUCTURE : Pay Grade 5 (Hiring Range $24.52 to $31.38. per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Academic-Computing-Specialist_JR101532": {
      "title": "Academic Computing Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nJob Description SUMMARY : The Academic Computing Specialist (ACS) works closely with faculty, staff, and students to ensure the classrooms and computers labs (public and departmental) provide an excellent instructional environment. Works to support the software and hardware needs for academic computing across the university campus. The Academic Computing Specialist (ATC) also works closely with other groups in Information Services (Help Desk, Media Support, Networking, Systems and Operations) to support and maintain academic computing. JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Provide technical support and assistance to faculty, staff, and students with regards to software applications in academic environments to include Installation and configuration of general and departmental specific software, Diagnose and resolve issues related to software and/or hardware. Build, deploy, and maintain computer images for Windows, Mac, and Linux operating systems. Maintain comprehensive and accurate statistics for hardware inventory, software allocation and usage, lab and classroom usage. Track and coordinate assigned asset replacement cycles. Supervise and train student assistants. Assist the manager in providing communication to the university community about outage and upgrades (5%). Work directly with vendors for technical support of software and hardware (10%). Collaborate with other information services departments (Media Support Services, Help Desk, Networking, and System Groups) to create and provide quality academic computing environments. QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge, skills & ability:  Excellent skills in all phases of desktop management Ability to read, comprehend, produce, and maintain technical documentation Ability to work both independently and within groups Expert knowledge of Windows and Mac operating systems, certifications preferred Some knowledge of Linux operating systems Excellent listening, interpersonal, written and oral communication skills SUPERVISION EXERCISED: This position will oversee a group of student assistants that work in public labs and provide technical support to the Academic Computing Services staff. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT: This position will work extensively with computer hardware and software. Individual must be able to sit and read a computer screen for extended periods. Frequently required to walk across campus. Ability to safely lift and carry computer equipment weighing up to 40 pounds without assistance. Occasionally work after hours and weekends as required to meet project deadlines and respond to critical situations. Education & experience : Bachelor’s degree or equivalent in education and experience Experience in supporting a multi-user computer environment Familiarity with Windows, Mac, and Linux operating systems, LANS, Windows and MAC Servers, Wireless technologies Work Schedule: Full-time; Nonexempt position Monday-Friday 8:30AM-5:00PM Pay Structure: Pay Grade 6 Hourly ($24.26 to $32.14 per hour)"
    },
    "https://richmond.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/staff_faculty/job/UR-Main-Campus/Recruiting-Coordinator-Professional-Education_JR101513": {
      "title": "Recruiting Coordinator Professional Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nJob Description Position Summary : Please attach a cover letter Recruiting Coordinator serves as the primary point of contact for prospective Professional Education students. The position supports enrollment growth by promoting Professional Education recruiting programs, responding to inquiries, engaging prospective students, identifying appropriate educational opportunities, and guiding individuals toward programs that align with their professional and educational goals.   The Coordinator develops a strong working knowledge of the Professional Education portfolio and consistently represents and promotes the unit through individual outreach, presentations, information sessions, community and professional events, and other engagement opportunities. The position manages incoming inquiries by phone, email, and in person; maintains prospective student relationships through the School's customer relationship management system (CRM); and supports continued engagement with current and former students by sharing relevant new offerings.   The Coordinator also supports the development of organizational and community partnerships, collaborates with the Marketing & Engagement team on enrollment-focused communications and outreach, assists with recruitment and engagement events, and provides program and current student support as needed.   JOB DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Enrollment and Student Engagement                                                                                                               Serves as the primary point of contact for individuals interested in Professional Education programs; manages the departmental email inbox; and responds to or routes phone, email, and in-person inquiries in a timely, professional, and helpful manner. Develops and maintains detailed knowledge of the Professional Education portfolio to answer questions, recommend appropriate programs, and create and deliver presentations and other informational resources. Collaborates with Senior Program Managers, the Associate Dean of Enrollment Management and Admissions (ADEMA), and the Marketing & Engagement team to develop and execute comprehensive enrollment and outreach plans, expand the prospective student pool, and identify new recruitment opportunities. Engages and guides prospective students through phone, email, individual meetings, presentations, information sessions, and other outreach activities; uses the School's CRM (Slate) to document, track, and advance prospective students through the enrollment process. Represents SPCS and the University at community and educational events, employment fairs, professional conferences, and other recruitment and outreach opportunities throughout the Richmond region, the Commonwealth, and beyond. Tracks and reports on enrollment and engagement activities and the effectiveness of recruitment strategies; recommends improvements; maintains related standard operating procedures (SOPs); and provides additional enrollment and engagement support as directed. Partnership Development                                                                                                               Develops and maintains a process and pipeline for researching and identifying businesses, employers, educational institutions, professional associations, and community organizations that may benefit from Professional Education programming, customized training, or other educational opportunities. Manages initial inbound and outbound partnership outreach and conducts preliminary conversations to understand organizational needs and assess alignment with Professional Education capabilities. Tracks partnership prospects, activities, and outcomes and transitions qualified opportunities to Senior Program Managers and/or the Director of Professional Education for relationship development, program design, and implementation. Communications                                                                                                               Supports enrollment-focused communications to prospective, current, and former students in collaboration with Senior Program Managers and the Marketing & Engagement team, including regular outreach and follow-up that encourages continued engagement and movement toward enrollment. Communicates program updates and changes to the Marketing & Engagement team to support accurate web and promotional content and assists with student and instructor communications, internal and external publications, and official announcements through Slate and other communication platforms. Reviews prospective student questions, feedback, interests, and inquiry trends; shares relevant insights with program and marketing staff; recommends potential growth areas; and provides additional communications support as directed. Marketing and Engagement Support                                                                                                               Collaborates with Senior Program Managers and the Marketing & Engagement team to support marketing and communication strategies that increase awareness, engagement, and enrollment in Professional Education programs. Provides program knowledge and prospective student insights to support print materials, digital advertising, email campaigns, presentations, social media content, feature stories, and other promotional efforts. Supports outreach through professional associations, employers, educational institutions, alumni, and community organizations; identifies opportunities to increase Professional Education visibility; and collaborates with the Marketing & Engagement team and ADEMA to evaluate and improve outreach and engagement strategies. Event Planning and Outreach                                                                                                               Plans, coordinates, and supports campus, community, organization-specific, and virtual events that promote Professional Education and engage prospective students, including tabling opportunities, information sessions, open houses, and program graduations. Serves as a point of contact for vendors, venues, professional organizations, and partner organizations; coordinates event logistics, materials, staffing, and technology; and represents Professional Education by directly engaging prospective students and community members. Evaluates and reports on the effectiveness of events as enrollment and engagement strategies and provides additional event support as directed. Assists Program Managers with program administration, instructor communication, and student support and serves as a resource who connects newly enrolled students with appropriate program staff and information. Coordinates logistics for Professional Education classes, orientations, instructor trainings, graduations, and other program activities. Provides basic classroom technology and logistical support to instructors and assists with the preparation and distribution of program materials and student communications. Identifies and recommends processes, tools, and practices that improve the student experience, enrollment processes, or departmental efficiency and provides additional program support as directed. SUPERVISION EXERCISED Occasional supervision of other Program Coordinators, Specialist, Student Employees, and Instructors WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL EFFORT Environment – Work may be required: On campus in School of Professional & Continued Studies office and other on campus locations or off campus venues or locations Exposures: Required to work outside on occasion, weather permitting Essential Personal Requirements: Comfortable greeting and speaking to the public Physically capable of setting up displays, display “tents,” tables, signage, and walking and standing for extended periods of time Must be able to move, lift and carry up to 30 pounds QUALIFICATIONS: Understanding of the operations/processes of higher education Knowledge of noncredit education, professional development, continuing education, workforce development, or related educational programming preferred. Strong organizational, project management, and follow-through skills. Ability to quickly develop and maintain detailed knowledge of a broad portfolio of Professional Education programs. Strong interpersonal and relationship-building skills, with the ability to engage effectively with prospective students, current students, employers, community organizations, and professional partners. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Strong presentation and public speaking skills. Ability to conduct proactive outreach and develop new prospective student and partnership opportunities. Ability to communicate educational opportunities clearly and match prospective students with programs appropriate to their needs and goals. Ability to manage multiple priorities, inquiries, projects, and events simultaneously. Ability to analyze enrollment, inquiry, engagement, and outreach data and use findings to improve strategies and processes. Ability to work independently while collaborating effectively across program, marketing, administrative, and leadership teams.  Software: Customer relationship management systems (CRM) (Slate) Learning management systems (LMS) (Canvas) Noncredit registration and e-commerce platforms, such as Enrole, Noodle, or Modern Campus Microsoft Office applications Zoom Survey and data collection software (Qualtrics) Email marketing and communication platforms Calendly Other technology used to support student engagement, reporting, enrollment, and program delivery Education & experience : Bachelor’s degree required Experience in higher education, continuing education, professional education, student engagement, enrollment management, recruitment, sales, community outreach, workforce development, or a related field preferred. Experience developing relationships with prospective students, customers, clients, community organizations, or business partners preferred. Experience with program administration, project management, event coordination, or partnership development preferred. Experience using CRM systems to manage prospective student, customer, or client relationships preferred. Schedule : University of Richmond office hours 8:30am – 5pm; Monday – Friday, 38.75 per week Required to work evenings and weekends as needed for special events   Salary Exempt Grade 5: $43,867 to $58,136"
    }
  },
